body:
Top-level `references` is a map of name → fetch-and-model definition, prefetched before the item runs and cached for reuse — useful for a shared auth token, or any lookup you don't want to re-fetch per field.

```json
"references": {
  "Token": {
    "connector_config": { "response_type": "json", "url": "https://auth.example.com/token", "server_config": { "method": "POST" } },
    "model": { "base_field": { "type": "string", "path": "access_token" } },
    "expire": 3600
  }
}
```

`expire` is in seconds — omitted means cached for the lifetime of the run/process, `0` means refetch every access. A reference can be used two ways:

1. **As an item's own source** — `connector_config.reference_config.name = "Token"` reuses the cached value directly instead of fetching again. See the [reference-as-source example](#/examples/reference-as-source).
2. **Inline via placeholder** — `{{{RefName=Token}}}` (whole value) or `{{{RefName=Token json.path}}}` (extract by path) anywhere placeholders are allowed — URLs, headers, bodies, templates. See the [Telegram briefing example](#/examples/telegram-briefing), which prefetches Hacker News + BTC price as references and interpolates both into one outbound message.
